    id:'opqb-t25-8', bank:'gradegain', module:'ophtho', chapter:'op-refract',
    stem:'Treatment of a –14D myope includes?',
    options:['Phakic IOL implantation','Clear lens extraction and IOL implantation','LASIK','All of the above','A + B'],
    answer:4,
    explanation:'**⚠️ This is the only five-option question in End Exam 5 (A–E), and its key is E → index 4.**\n\n**–14 D is high myopia, and the deck\'s cut-off is printed.** `L7,8) Refractive errors` slide 45 divides myopia into *"**Simple myopia:** less than 6 dioptres"* and *"**High myopia** (Progressive – Degenerative – Malignant): can reach more than 20 dioptres"*. Fourteen dioptres of minus sits well inside the high group.\n\n**The treatment tree, verbatim from slide 47:**\n\n> **Treatment**\n> ├── **Conservative** — Glasses (concave or minus lenses) · Contact lenses\n> └── **Surgical**\n> ..├── **Corneal** — **PRK** · **LASIK**\n> ..└── **Lenticular** — **Phakic IOL — <40 years** · **Clear lens extraction — > 40 years**\n\nThe bank\'s key takes the whole **lenticular** branch and rejects the corneal one. That is the right shape: a very high myope needs more dioptric change than the cornea can safely give up, so the correction is placed **inside the eye** — either an extra lens in front of the natural one (phakic IOL) or the natural lens exchanged for a powered implant.\n\n**⚠️ Why LASIK is excluded — and the number IS printed, just not in this deck.** `L7,8) Refractive errors` names PRK, LASIK, phakic IOL and clear lens extraction and **describes none of them**; its own summary records *"no patient-selection criterion beyond the <40 / >40 age split on p47, no contraindication (thin cornea, keratoconus, unstable refraction), and no complication of any refractive procedure."* The ceiling is in the **cornea** deck instead. `L15,16) Cornea`, comparing the laser procedures, gives LASIK a *"**Wide range: myopia up to −10 D**"* — and **–14 D is past it.** The same deck explains why: *"Ectasia is the most feared long-term complication of LASIK"*, and its risk factors are *"forme fruste keratoconus, thin cornea, young age, **high myopia**, low residual stromal bed (**RSB <300 µm**)"*, with its safety checklist demanding *"Central thickness ≥500 µm"* and *"RSB target ≥300 µm post-op"*. The deeper the ablation the correction needs, the less stroma is left under the flap — so beyond about −10 D the cornea runs out of tissue before the refraction runs out of dioptres, and the correction has to be placed **inside the eye** instead.\n\n**⚠️ THE OPTION MENU IS INTERNALLY INCONSISTENT — recorded, and the key is NOT disputed.** The bank prints both **D. All of the above** and **E. A + B**. If D were correct, E could not be, and vice versa; the two cannot coexist as honest options in one menu. The bank keys **E**, which is also the medically defensible one, and by keying E the bank asserts that its own option D is false. **`answer` stays at index 4, exactly as printed.**\n\n**A second, smaller defect in the same menu.** The deck separates A and B by **age** — phakic IOL under 40, clear lens extraction over 40 — so for any single patient only one of them is on offer. The stem gives **no age**, which is what lets "A + B" be read as *the lenticular options* rather than *two operations for this person*. Read as a list of what the lenticular branch contains, E is right; read as a plan for one patient, it is one procedure too many. The bank means the former.\n\n**Why each distractor is wrong:**\n\n| Option | Verdict |\n|---|---|\n| **Phakic IOL implantation** *(alone)* | True but incomplete — it is the **under-40** half of the lenticular branch. It preserves accommodation, which is exactly why it is the young patient\'s option. |\n| **Clear lens extraction and IOL implantation** *(alone)* | True but incomplete — the **over-40** half, offered once accommodation is largely spent (slide 75 puts the accommodative amplitude at *"3 dioptres by the age of 40"*), so little is lost by removing a clear lens. |\n| **LASIK** | The corneal branch, and the one procedure on the menu that does not belong to a –14 D eye: `L15,16) Cornea` caps LASIK at *"myopia up to −10 D"* and names high myopia a risk factor for post-LASIK ectasia. Including it is what makes **D** false. |\n| **All of the above** | False **because** it contains LASIK, and the bank\'s own key of E says so. |\n\n**SOURCE DEFECTS, recorded not repaired:** the stem prints an **en dash (U+2013)** rather than a hyphen-minus in *"a –14D myope"* — it is a minus sign, read off the page image at magnification — and ends *"includes?"*. Both kept as printed.\n\nWritten for this bank — ophthalmology qb.pdf prints no explanation here.',
    objective:'Route a high myope to the lenticular refractive options — phakic IOL under 40, clear lens extraction over 40 — rather than to corneal ablation, and read a menu whose "all of the above" and "A + B" options contradict each other.',
    source:'ophthalmology qb.pdf p.148, Q8 (Grade Gain, "End Exam 5"). ⚠️ FIVE OPTIONS (A–E), the only such question in the section; printed key E → index 4. ⚠️ SOURCE DEFECTS, not repaired: en dash (U+2013) before 14D, stem ends "includes?", and options D ("All of the above") and E ("A + B") are mutually inconsistent — the key of E is recorded, never disputed. Supporting: `L7,8) Refractive errors` sl.45 (simple <6 D, high >20 D), sl.47 (treatment tree with the <40 / >40 lenticular split), sl.75 (accommodative amplitude 3 D at 40) — that deck describes no refractive procedure and prints no dioptric limit; `L15,16) Cornea` (Laser Refractive Procedures comparison — LASIK "Wide range: myopia up to −10 D"; ectasia risk factors including high myopia; safe-surgery checklist, central thickness ≥500 µm, RSB ≥300 µm).'
