    explanation:'**The book prints the definition inside a parenthesis, and the parenthesis has two halves.** `ch06-conjunctiva.txt` printed p.92, describing the corneal signs of **active trachoma**, lists *"Superior epithelial keratitis"* and then *"**pannus** formation (**superficial corneal vascularization and cellular infiltration**)"*.\n\n**The whole question is whether you kept the second half.** Options A and B are the same definition with and without *"cellular infiltration"* — and that is the difference between a description of pannus and a description of any vascularised cornea. Pannus is not merely vessels on the cornea: it is a **sheet of vessels advancing together with an infiltrate of inflammatory cells**, the two moving as one front. `L6) Conjunctiva` slide 28 shows precisely that and nothing else — *a sheet of fine, straight, radial vessels crossing the limbus from the superior conjunctiva and invading the upper cornea, **with a hazy grey infiltration of the cornea at their advancing edge***. The vessels and the haze arrive together.\n\n**Why superior matters, and why the deck teaches it by photograph.** Trachomatous pannus comes down from **above**, because the disease lives on the **upper** tarsal conjunctiva — that is where the follicles are and where the scarring forms. The book\'s own figure note records that the direction *"is carried by the picture alone"*, with no label or arrow. A vascularised **lower** cornea is not trachoma.\n\n**Why each distractor is wrong:**\n\n| Option | Verdict |\n|---|---|\n| **Superficial vascularization** | ⚠️ **Half the printed definition, and the trap.** It keeps *superficial* — which is correct, pannus is a sub-epithelial and superficial-stromal process — but drops **cellular infiltration**, which is what makes the front grey rather than merely red. Superficial vascularisation alone happens in contact-lens hypoxia and in chronic limbal irritation of any kind; it is a sign, not a lesion. Option A carries both halves and is therefore the complete answer. |\n| **Corneal edema** | A different process entirely — fluid in the stroma from endothelial failure or raised pressure, with no vessels and no cells. `L15,16) Cornea` puts corneal oedema under disciform keratitis and endothelial disease. |\n| **Corneal scarring** | The **sequel**, not the lesion. When trachomatous pannus regresses it leaves the veil of haze `L6) Conjunctiva` slide 29 labels *"Trachomatous **Nebula ex Pannus**"* — a band of faint grey haze across the upper cornea with *"a few residual empty vessels"* where the dense vascular sheet used to be. The name itself says it: nebula **out of** pannus. Answering "scarring" names the scar and misses the active disease that made it. |\n\n**Clinical bottom line.** Grey-fronted vessels marching onto the **upper** cornea from the limbus, in an eye with upper tarsal follicles and mucopurulent discharge, is active trachoma — and the pannus is one of the two things that will eventually take the vision, the other being the trichiasis and cicatricial entropion of the cicatricial stage.\n\n*(Secondary chapter: `op-conj` — the definition is printed under trachoma, a conjunctival disease.)*\n\nWritten for this bank — ophthalmology qb.pdf prints no explanation here.',
    objective:'Define pannus as superficial corneal vascularisation together with cellular infiltration, and distinguish it from bare vascularisation, from oedema, and from the nebula it leaves behind.',
    source:'ophthalmology qb.pdf p.149, Q13 (Grade Gain, "End Exam 5"). Supporting: `ch06-conjunctiva.txt` printed p.92 (active trachoma — "pannus formation (superficial corneal vascularization and cellular infiltration)"); `L6) Conjunctiva` sl.28 (Trachomatous Pannus — radial vessels from the superior limbus with grey infiltration at the advancing edge) and sl.29 (Trachomatous Nebula ex Pannus).'
