    stem:'Pachymetry is used to:',
    options:['Measure corneal thickness','Measure corneal curvature','Test corneal sensitivity','Measure visual field'],
    answer:0,
    explanation:'**The word says it: *pachy-* is thick, *-metry* is measurement.** `ch20-drugs.txt` printed p.258 settles it in a single line — among the **uses of topical anaesthetics** it lists *"**Corneal thickness measurement (pachymetry)**"*, alongside tonometry, foreign-body removal, suture removal and gonioscopy. The anaesthetic is needed because the classic pachymeter probe touches the cornea.\n\n**Where the number is actually used.** `L15,16) Cornea` makes pachymetry one of the mandatory checks before refractive surgery — *"Mandatory pre-op assessment: corneal topography, **pachymetry**, pupil size, tear film, refraction stability (≥1 yr)"* — and then gives the thresholds that make the measurement matter:\n\n> • *"Pachymetry: central thickness **<500 µm** is a relative contraindication; **RSB <300 µm** = absolute contraindication for LASIK."*\n> • Safe-surgery checklist: *"✓ Central thickness **≥500 µm**"* · *"✓ RSB target **≥300 µm** post-op"*\n\nThe reason is ectasia — *"the most feared long-term complication of LASIK"* — whose risk factors the deck lists as forme fruste keratoconus, **thin cornea**, young age, high myopia and a low residual stromal bed. Pachymetry is how the thin cornea is caught before the laser is fired, which is why it is the test that decides whether an eye may have LASIK at all.\n\n**Why each distractor is wrong:**\n\n| Option | Verdict |\n|---|---|\n| **Measure corneal curvature** | That is **keratometry** and **corneal topography** — two different instruments, listed separately from pachymetry in the same pre-op line. Topography is the one that *"RULE OUT ectasia"* by pattern (*"asymmetric bowtie, inferior steepening = contraindication"*); pachymetry rules it out by thickness. `L18) Cataract II` uses keratometry for a third purpose again — IOL power calculation. Curvature and thickness are measured by different machines and answer different questions. |\n| **Test corneal sensitivity** | Done at the bedside with a wisp of cotton wool, or with an aesthesiometer; no pachymeter is involved. It matters in exactly the diseases where the fifth nerve is damaged — `L23) Ocular manifestations of systemic diseases` flags *"loss of normal corneal sensation"* in herpes zoster, and `L15,16) Cornea` lists neurotrophic keratopathy among the HZO complications. |\n| **Measure visual field** | Perimetry, and nothing to do with the cornea. |\n\n**⚠️ Where this is NOT taught.** `ch01-examination.txt`, the examination chapter, records *"Keratometry, corneal topography, **pachymetry**, and central corneal thickness as an IOP correction factor"* in its list of subjects that are **absent** from the chapter. So the fact is examinable from the drugs chapter and the cornea deck, and not from the chapter on examination where a reader would look for it first.\n\nWritten for this bank — ophthalmology qb.pdf prints no explanation here.',
    objective:'Identify pachymetry as the measurement of corneal thickness, name its 500 µm and 300 µm refractive-surgery thresholds, and separate it from keratometry, aesthesiometry and perimetry.',
    source:'ophthalmology qb.pdf p.149, Q14 (Grade Gain, "End Exam 5"). Supporting: `ch20-drugs.txt` printed p.258 (uses of topical anaesthetics — "Corneal thickness measurement (pachymetry)"); `L15,16) Cornea` (refractive surgery pre-op must-checks; "central thickness <500 µm is a relative contraindication; RSB <300 µm = absolute contraindication for LASIK"; ectasia risk factors). ⚠️ `ch01-examination.txt` lists pachymetry among the topics ABSENT from the examination chapter.'
