    answer:0,
    explanation:'**"Red overlying skin" is the phrase that makes this the ACUTE disease, and the acute and chronic forms are treated in opposite ways.** `ch04-lacrimal-system.txt` printed p.67 defines dacryocystitis as *"Acute or chronic infection of the lacrimal sac"*, *"usually secondary to **Obstruction of the nasolacrimal duct**"*, with *"staphylococci or streptococci"* as the commonest organisms. Under **A. Acute Dacryocystitis** its signs are *"**Very tender and tense swelling with overlying skin erythema**"*, which *"can progress to **abscess formation**"*, with *"**Preseptal cellulitis** may be present"* — and symptoms of pain, redness and swelling **below the medial canthal area**, epiphora, and *"Fever may be present"*. The stem is that sign, quoted.\n\n**The printed treatment, in order:**\n\n> ▪ Warm compresses and nasal decongestants.\n> ▪ **Systemic and topical antibiotics.**\n> ▪ Once the infection resolves, **consider dacryocystorhinostomy DCR**\n> ▪ For abscess : incision & drainage *(Tutorial)*\n\n**⚠️ A DIVERGENCE, recorded rather than corrected — the book prints BOTH routes.** The line reads *"**Systemic and topical** antibiotics"*, so option B is not absent from the printed treatment; it is half of a single printed item that the bank has split into two competing options. The key of A is nonetheless the defensible half, and for a structural reason: the infection sits **inside an obstructed sac**, behind a blocked nasolacrimal duct, with cellulitis in the tissues around it. Topical drops reach the ocular surface and the tear film — they do not reach the lumen of a closed, obstructed sac, and they do not treat a preseptal cellulitis or a fever. **The systemic route is the one doing the work; the topical is adjunctive.** *(That drops cannot penetrate an obstructed sac is __not taken from the course material__ — the book prints the two routes together and never ranks them.)*\n\n**Why each distractor is wrong:**\n\n| Option | Verdict |\n|---|---|\n| **Localized antibiotics** | ⚠️ Printed, but as the lesser half of *"Systemic and topical antibiotics"* — see above. Alone, it treats the surface of an eye whose problem is a walled-off abscess cavity beside it. |\n| **Dacryocystorhinostomy (DCR)** | The **definitive** treatment, and explicitly **deferred**: *"**Once the infection resolves**, consider dacryocystorhinostomy DCR."* It is the right operation at the wrong moment — DCR opens the sac into the nose (the chapter describes *"surgically connecting the lacrimal sa[c] and the **middle meatus** of the nasal cavity with placement of either temporary or permanent tubes"*), and operating through actively infected, inflamed tissue is how a fistula is made. Note that for **chronic** dacryocystitis, printed p.68, DCR is the whole treatment — which is why the word *acute* in the stem changes the answer. |\n| **Probing** | Belongs to a different disease and a different patient. Probing is for **congenital nasolacrimal duct obstruction** — the chapter\'s four-rung infant ladder is massage with topical antibiotics before age one (*"85% resolve within the first year"*), probing after age one, repeatable **only once**, then silastic intubation, then DCR — and the chapter ends the section with a flat statement: *"**N.B: Probing is not curative in adults.**"* |\n\n**Clinical bottom line.** Hot, red, exquisitely tender swelling below the medial canthus: systemic antibiotics now, warm compresses, drain it if it points, and book the DCR for after it has settled — because the duct is still blocked and it will come back.\n\n**SOURCE DEFECT, recorded not repaired:** the stem is a **sentence fragment with no interrogative** — *"Dacryocystitis with red overlying skin. Treatment:"*. Kept as printed.\n\nWritten for this bank — ophthalmology qb.pdf prints no explanation here.',
    objective:'Treat acute dacryocystitis with systemic antibiotics first, defer DCR until the infection settles, and recognise probing as belonging to congenital nasolacrimal duct obstruction.',
